Chiral Reticular Self-Assembly of Achiral AIEgen into Optically Pure MOFs with Dual Mechano-Switchable Circularly Polarized Luminescence
Circularly polarized luminescence (CPL) is the excited state properties of a chiral system[1] and has wide applications in advanced chiroptical materials.[2] Generally, both emissive and chiral components should be harmonically integrated, whereas the combination manner of the components played an important role in developing CPL materials.[1] As illustrated in Scheme 1a, many CPL active materials were developed through rational designs of either covalent[3] or non-covalent[4] approach. Therefore, CPL properties could be tuned through either emissive or chiral motifs, or their conjunction. However, it is still a great challenge to achieve an efficient manipulation of CPL activities. For example, tough and tedious syntheses were often inevitable in covalent chiral luminophores,[3] while efficient chiraltransfer routes was necessary for noncovalent systems.[4] Particularly, it is often difficult to achieve enough efficiency and stability for the self-assembled CPL materials.[1a, 1b, 4b, 5] To fabricate efficient CPL materials, new pathway of chirality transfer and regulation is still in urgent need.
Reticular chemistry[6] offered a rich opportunity to integrate different organic units to produce stable functional materials. If chiral units and luminophores were connected through a chiral reticular self-assembly,[7] it was expected that the chiral framework would enable a different manner of chirality transfer for CPL materials.[7d, 7e] In addition, the chiral framework could experience either global or local dynamics under external stimuli to fabricate responsive functional materials[6a]. The discovery of aggregation-induced emission (AIE)[8] greatly expanded the scope of luminescent materials and the intrinsic properties of AIE luminogen (AIEgen) promised multiple ways to emissive regulation.[8] Thus, AIEgen would be a superb candidate as the emissive component for CPL materials.[9]
Herein, we constructed optically pure single-crystal MOFs[7] with excellent CPL performance from an achiral AIEgen, tetrakis(4-pyridylphenyl)ethylene (TPyPE) and chiral camphoric acid $(\mathsf{C a m})^{[10]}$ through the chiral reticular self-assembly with Cd(II) ion (Scheme 1b). Such strategy highlighted several merits: (1) Separated chiral and luminescent motifs made it versatile for the preparation of CPL materials. (2) It achieved luminescence properties via frame-enabled emission (FEE) of AIEgen and chiral functions via through-space chirality transfer (TSCT) spontaneously for CPL materials. (3) The framework allowed a certain dynamics of AIEgen rotors to modulate CPL activities against external stimuli. As an illustration, while the asprepared AIEgen MOF emitted cyanine CPL, bluish violet and yellowish green CPL were obtained after ultrasonication and grinding, respectively. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first report on the optically pure AIEgen MOFs[11] with a dual mechano-switchable CD/FL/CPL.
The optically pure AIEgen MOF was synthesized from a typical solvothermal reaction. Briefly, TPyPE, Cam and ${\mathsf{C d}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2}$ $4H_{2}O$ were dissolved into mixed solvent (DMF/ethano ${\cal{I}}{\sf H}_{2}{\sf O}$ ) to react at $95~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ for 24h. Colorless transparent, hexagonal crystals were obtained (for details, see the Experimental Section in the Supporting Information). Scanning electronic microscopy (SEM) showed the size of the crystals ranged from 100 to $300~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Fig. 1a, S1).
Single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SXRD) of D-MOF revealed that TPyPE and D-Cam were self-assembled with Cd(II) ions into a chiral hexagonal space group, $\mathsf{P}6_{2}22$ , which is the same as that of the natural $\upbeta$ -quartz[10b] (Fig. 1, Table S1). From the 001 plane, a perfect hexagonal packing pattern was apparently visualized (Fig. 1d). Optical microscopy showed a beautiful diffraction pattern at the macroscopic scale, which further demonstrated the hexagonal nature of the crystals (Fig. 1d, inset). It could be found that D-Cam were packed into a righthanded form via the coordination with Cd(II) ions (Fig. 1b). There were two kinds of TPyPE molecules, the light purple one was parallel and the cyanine one perpendicular to the ab plane. Accordingly, the light purple TPyPE were organized into a righthanded form at the ab plane (Fig. 1c). Meanwhile, the cyanine TPyPE were in a right-handed packing along the $c$ axis (Fig. 1d). In fact, the polyhedral of Cd(II) ions, D-Cam, and TPyPE  were all in a right-handed packing mode (Fig. 1e). That is, achiral TPyPE formed optically pure single-crystal MOF via TSCT from D-Cam.
When D-Cam was changed into L-Cam, hexagonal L-MOF crystals was also fabricated (Fig. S2). Single-crystal XRD of LMOF revealed a chiral hexagonal space group, $\mathsf{P6}_{4}22$ , with a left-handed packing manner for ${\mathsf{C d}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ ions, L-Cam, and TPyPE (Fig. S3, Table S1), which were mirrors of those in D-MOF (Fig 1f, S4). The powder XRD spectra of $D-$ and L-MOF showed the same diffraction pattern, suggesting the similar layered packing mode (Fig. S5,S6). These results indicate that the lamellar single-crystal AIEgen MOFs with either right-handed or lefthanded chirality were successfully fabricated through the chiral reticular self-assembly.
Circular dichroism (CD) was employed to evaluate the chiroptical properties of the AIEgen MOFs. While TPyPE showed no CD signals (Fig. S7), mirrored Cotton effects at 343 nm corresponded to the $\pi-\pi^{\star}$ transition of TPyPE was recorded, negative and positive signals for D- and L-MOF, respectively (Fig. 1g,S8, Table S2). The $\mathfrak{g}_{\mathtt{a b s}}$ of D-MOF was ${\cdot}2.3^{\star}10^{-3}$ , of which the absolute value was comparable to that of L-MOF $(2.6^{\star}10^{-3})$ . These results clearly evidenced the chiral nature of D/L-Cam transferred to achiral TPyPE via efficient TSCT. On the other hand, TPyPE was non-emissive in solution, but emitted at $464\ensuremath{\mathrm{~\textrm~{~~}~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{nm}}$ in the solid state (Fig. S7). However, a strong luminescence peaked at $452\ n m$ in MOFs (Fig. 2a,c) was observed. Such luminescence, without the aggregation of TPyPE, was due to the restriction of the intramolecular vibration and partial rotation of TPyPE in the framework (FEE). The combination of FEE with TSCT in the same framework endowed the AIEgen MOFs strong cyanine CPL at $463~\mathsf{n m}$ , negative and positive for D- and L-MOF, respectively. The high dissymmetrical factor $\mathsf{g}_{\mathsf{l u m}}$ was up to 0.012 (Fig. S8), which was ca. 5 times large than $\mathfrak{g}_{\mathsf{a b s}}$ , owing to the dense packing and fixation of the AIEgen in the crystalline MOFs.[1d]
Besides FEE and TSCT phenomena, the framework virtue also made it possible to tune either local or global dynamics[6a] of the AIEgen rotors, consequently to regulate the luminescence of AIEgen MOFs. This feature further provided a switchable CPL materials.[2a-d, 9a, 9b] Su’s group has reported the mechanical stimulus triggered stepwise luminescence response of AIEgen MOF.[12] Here, by means of chiral reticular self-assembly, a dual mechano-stimuli-responsive FL/CPL material was developed. Such FL/CPL switch has not been reported previously.
Taken D-MOF as an example, a switchable color under stimuli was observed (Fig. 2). The as-prepared colorless crystals of D-MOF emitted strong cyanine blue light $(452\ \mathsf{n m})$ . After full grinding, colorless crystals tuned into yellow powders and emitted at 512 nm with a red shift. We further exploited ultrasound to trigger the local rotation of AIEgen rotors in MOF crystals for the first time. In contrast to grinding, the emissive band of ultrasound treated DMF suspension of the as-prepared MOF blue shifted from 452 to $434\mathsf{n m}$ (Fig. 2a,c).
Moreover, the intensity of both grinding and ultrasonication could finely regulate the emissive properties including FL and CPL. The emissive bands were in between that of the original and fully grinded MOFs while slightly pressed, while the emission gradually blue shift with either time or strength of the ultrasonication (Fig. $\$9$ , Table S3). The largest red and blue shifts of FL bands was up to $61~\mathsf{n m}$ (from 452 to $513\mathsf{n m}$ ) and 25 nm (from 452 to $427~\mathsf{n m})$ , respectively. Accordingly, the CPL performance could also be modulated from 463 to ca. $530~\mathsf{n m}$ $(\Delta\lambda\approx67\ \mathsf{n m})$ by grinding and to 427 nm $(\Delta\lambda=29\ \mathrm{nm})$ by ultrasonication (Fig. S9).
Most Interestingly, when the grinded powder was suspended into DMF for relaxation (Fig. 2a,c), the emissive color could fully recover to cyanine blue overnight. The violet ultrasonicated MOF crystals tuned to cyanine blue once DMF was removed (Fig. S10,S11, Table S4). For the first time, a dual mechanoswitchable FL/CPL was achieved by grinding and ultrasonication.
Powder XRD (PXRD) revealed a layered nature of MOF structures with a d-spacing of $1.60\ \mathsf{n m}$ (001) and 0.80 (002), which were corresponding to the distance of 1.595 and 0.798 nm of TPyPE layers and $\mathsf{C d}(\mathsf{I I})$ ions layers in SXRD, respectively (Fig. 3a, S6). After grinding, the 001 and 002-peak tuned very weak, suggesting the layer distances became closer each other. Consequently, red shifts of absorption (UV-vis/CD) and emission (FL/CPL) were observed and the $\mathfrak{g}_{\mathtt{a b s}}$ significantly decreased from $-2.3^{\star}10^{-3}$ $(343\ \mathsf{n m})$ to $-3.2^{\star}10^{-4}$ $(456\ \mathsf{n m})$ , suggesting the deformation of the chiral coordination framework (Fig. 2f, Table S3). Remarkably, the intensity of 001 and 002-peak recovered after relaxation in DMF, indicating the layer distances were restored. Subsequently, the FL/CPL properties were recovered. That is, despite of the damage of the macroscopic single-crystal structures by the destructive grinding stimuli (Fig. S12), the microscopic chiral coordination frameworks were retained and maintained the reversible global dynamics of the AIEgen rotors, which further caused reversible FL/CPL switch.
On the other hand, PXRD pattern of the ultrasonicated and relaxed MOF was close to that of the as-prepared MOF, suggesting that the layered structures of the frameworks were perfectly preserved (Fig. 3a). However, the gabs drastically dropped from $-2.3^{\ast}10^{-3}$ $(343~\mathsf{n m})$ to $-1.2^{\star}10^{-4}$ ( $410\ \mathsf{n m}$ , Fig. 2f, Table S3). This suggested that the local phenyl orders were severely disturbed while the whole chiral framework unchanged. Therefore, ultrasonication triggered the local dynamics of the AIEgen rotors as a nondestructive mechanical stimulus to realize reversible FL/CPL switch.
Single-crystal XRD unveiled in as-prepared single-crystal MOF, the AIEgen rotors close packed in the chiral framework with orderly orientated phenyl rings and a dihedral angle of $29.08^{\circ}$ between adjacent phenyl rings (Fig. 3a)[11a, 11b], a large torsion owing to steric hindrance between phenyl rings was well immobilized by the framework. Consequently, strong FL/CPL with a high fluorescence quantum yield (QY, $\Phi=43\%$ ) and a life time $\tau=1.71$ ns (Fig. 3c, S13) was obtained.[11b] After grinding, the distance between TPyPE tuned closer, leading to a smaller dihedral angle $(<30^{\circ}$ , more flatten) with an even higher torsion due to the severe deformation of the framework,[8d] which was further evidenced by the fact that the split pyridyl chemical shift of TPyPE (carbon a in Fig. 3b) in MOF was close to that of the amorphous TPyPE solids after grinding. Therefore, the red shifted emission (yellowish green FL/CPL) with an improved QY (up to $65\%$ ) and a longer life time $\mathit{\Omega}(\tau\ =\ 4.78$ ns)[8d, 11b] was obtained (Fig. 3c, S13). After ultrasonication, the tension of the framework was released via a more twisted phenyl conformation $\mathrm{~\(>~\30^{\circ}~}$ , more perpendicular). This was evidenced by the dramatically lower field chemical shift of phenyl rings (carbon atom 7,5,2,3 in Fig. 3b) and a little d-spacing increase from 1.60 to $1.62\ \mathsf{n m}$ (Fig. 3a). As a result, a bluish violet FL/CPL with a dropped QY $(19\%)$ and a shorter life time $(\tau=1.27$ ns) was recorded (Fig. 3c, S13).[11b]
In addition, $^{13}\mathsf{C}$ CP-MAS NMR spectra showed a higher field chemical shift of the TPyPE vinyl carbon (carbon h in Fig. 3b) in the MOFs compared to the amorphous solid of TPyPE, which evidenced the orthogonal packing of TPyPE molecules in the layered framework (Fig. 3a,S6). Furthermore, the chemical shift kept unchanged after either ultrasonication or grinding, further suggesting the chiral framework were preserved by the global scissors-like spring motions under grinding and the local phenyl rotation along the biphenyl axis under ultrasonication. Owing to such different stimuli mechanism, the glum $(-0.58^{\star}10^{-3}$ , $513\mathsf{n m};$ of grinded MOF was lower than the $9\vert\mathrm{um}\ (-3.73^{\star}10^{-3}$ , 442 nm) of ultrasonicated MOF (Fig. $_{29,\mathsf{S}11}$ , Table S4).
It should be noted that the orderly orientated phenyl rings in as-prepared single-crystal structure was responsible for the large $\mathsf{g}_{\mathsf{l u m}}$ $-1.2^{\star}10^{-2}$ , $423\mathsf{n m}$ values, which disappeared once by both mechano-stimuli due to the disturbance. However, after relaxation the $\mathsf{g}_{\mathsf{l u m}}$ values were back to the same level as that of as-prepared MOF $(-7.8^{\star}10^{-3}$ , $463~\mathsf{n m};$ , $-2.88^{\star}10^{-3}$ $(463\mathsf{n m})$ and - $3.69^{\star}10^{-3}$ $(463\:\:\mathsf{n m})$ for ultrasonicated and grinded MOFs, respectively (Fig. 2g,S11, Table S4). These results further provided strong support that although the grinding was a deconstructive stimulus and ultrasonication non-deconstructive, the microscopic coordination framework was retained after both mechanical stimuli, and their dynamics at the microscopic level maintained the reversibility of the mechano-responsive FL/CPL performance.
In summary, we proposed a chiral reticular self-assembly strategy to fabricate optically pure single-crystal MOFs from achiral AIEgen. We further demonstrated that the framework enabled emission and through-space chirality transfer were responsible for the strong CPL activity of the AIEgen MOFs, while the dual mechano-switchable CD/FL/CPL could be ascribed to the reversible manipulation of either local or global dynamic of the AIEgen rotors in the chiral frameworks under external mechano-stimuli. Especially, we employed ultrasound to modulate the local dynamics of AIEgen rotors and thus nondestructively to regulate CD/FL/CPL in MOF crystals for the first time. This work provided an insightful view of AIE mechanism, as well as an efficient and versatile strategy for the preparation of reversibly switchable CPL materials.
